WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.226
Secure architecture starts by knowing what
data the service handles and why that

2
00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:04.964
data matters.

3
00:00:05.044 --> 00:00:08.058
A Go service may process public records,
internal business data,

4
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:11.316
personal information, credentials, tokens,
session identifiers,

5
00:00:11.396 --> 00:00:13.752
configuration values, and operational
metadata.

6
00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:17.559
Each category deserves a handling rule
that engineers can understand and apply.

7
00:00:17.639 --> 00:00:20.488
Secrets, tokens, keys, session
identifiers, personal data,

8
00:00:20.568 --> 00:00:24.185
and business-sensitive records should not
be treated like ordinary strings.

9
00:00:24.265 --> 00:00:27.717
They may need restricted logging, limited
serialization, narrow access,

10
00:00:27.797 --> 00:00:29.770
careful storage, and explicit ownership.

11
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.412
The code should make sensitive data paths
visible instead of relying on every

12
00:00:33.492 --> 00:00:35.191
developer to remember hidden rules.

13
00:00:35.271 --> 00:00:38.504
Configuration should separate safe
defaults from sensitive values.

14
00:00:38.584 --> 00:00:41.981
A timeout default or feature flag default
is different from a signing key,

15
00:00:42.061 --> 00:00:44.089
database password, or service credential.

16
00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:47.895
Required configuration should be validated
before the service starts handling

17
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:51.702
traffic, so missing or malformed values
fail clearly during startup rather than

18
00:00:51.782 --> 00:00:52.878
during a user workflow.

19
00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:54.821
Logging is a common data-handling risk.

20
00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:57.367
Logs should support operations and
investigations,

21
00:00:57.447 --> 00:01:00.735
but they should not leak secrets,
credentials, tokens, personal data,

22
00:01:00.815 --> 00:01:01.911
or sensitive payloads.

23
00:01:01.991 --> 00:01:05.059
Useful logs often include stable
identifiers, event categories,

24
00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:07.057
request correlation, dependency names,

25
00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:09.877
and failure reasons without recording
entire raw objects.

26
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:12.368
Encryption responsibilities should also be
clear.

27
00:01:12.448 --> 00:01:15.407
The architecture should identify what is
protected in transit,

28
00:01:15.487 --> 00:01:18.775
what is protected at rest, and which
platform, database, service mesh,

29
00:01:18.855 --> 00:01:21.650
load balancer, or application component
owns each control.

30
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.196
If the responsibility is assumed but not
documented,

31
00:01:24.276 --> 00:01:27.016
teams can miss gaps during migration or
incident response.

32
00:01:27.096 --> 00:01:30.165
Data handling rules should be visible in
code review, deployment,

33
00:01:30.245 --> 00:01:31.505
and operational runbooks.

34
00:01:31.585 --> 00:01:34.435
Reviewers should know when sensitive
fields are introduced.

35
00:01:34.515 --> 00:01:37.583
Operators should know where secrets come
from and how they rotate.

36
00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:40.677
Developers should know which response
models are safe to expose.

37
00:01:40.757 --> 00:01:44.100
Secure data handling is a lifecycle
practice, not a single library call.
